      "latency_ms": 5307.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 1061,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, forming the molecule **H₂O**. It is one of the most essential and abundant substances on Earth, playing a critical role in sustaining life and shaping the planet's environment. Here's a breakdown of its key characteristics:\n\n### **Chemical Composition**\n- **Molecular Structure**: Water is a polar molecule, with oxygen (more electronegative) pulling electrons toward itself, creating a partial negative charge on the oxygen and partial positive charges on the hydrogens. This polarity allows water to form **hydrogen bonds**, which influence its unique properties.\n\n### **Physical Properties**\n- **States of Matter**: Water exists as a **liquid** at standard temperature and pressure (room temperature), but it can also be a **solid** (ice) when frozen or a **gas** (water vapor) when heated. \n- **Unique Properties**: \n  - **High Specific Heat**: It absorbs and releases heat slowly, helping regulate temperature in organisms and the environment.\n  - **Cohesion and Adhesion**: Molecules stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion), enabling processes like capillary action in plants.\n  - **Solvent Ability**: Often called the \"universal solvent,\" it dissolves many substances, facilitating chemical reactions in living systems.\n\n### **Biological Importance**\n- **Life-Sustaining**: All known forms of life require water. It is a major component of cells, blood, and bodily fluids, aiding in nutrient transport, waste removal, and metabolic processes.\n- **Chemical Reactions**: Water participates in hydrolysis (breaking down molecules) and dehydration synthesis (building molecules), essential for life.\n\n### **Environmental Role**\n- **Hydrological Cycle**: Water cycles through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff, distributing heat and shaping weather patterns.\n- **Ecosystems**: It supports aquatic life, shapes landscapes (e.g., rivers, glaciers), and influences climate.\n\n### **Other Key Traits**\n- **Appearance**: Pure water is **colorless, odorless, and tasteless**. In large quantities, it appears blue due to light absorption.\n- **Abundance**: Covers about **71% of Earth's surface**, though only a small fraction is fresh and accessible.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple yet extraordinary molecule whose unique properties make it indispensable for life, environmental stability, and countless natural and industrial processes."
